title: Platform
kind: overview
aliases: platforms portability windows macos
summary: Linux is the platform metafolder runs on; the data model is portable, and the code is written for macOS and Windows where it can be, but neither is built or tested.

The ''data'' is platform-independent: paths are stored relative to the repository
root, `/`-separated, as the exact bytes of their names ([[Path representation]]), so a
repository created on one machine can be loaded on another — a repository on a
removable drive, say ([[Moving a repository]]).

The ''programs'' are another matter. What runs where, today:

|!Piece |!Linux |!macOS |!Windows |
|daemon, `mf` |built and tested |not built: written with Unix calls only (`flock`, `statvfs`, Unix sockets), no port work done |does not compile: those calls have no fallback |
|watcher |the inotify source, or the fanotify broker ([[Watch sources and regimes]]) |the `notify` source would place one registration per directory |same |
|`metafolder-watchd` |yes |no (fanotify is Linux-only) |no |
|GUI |yes |refuses to start: it needs `bwrap` ([[Sandboxing the media decoders]]) |same |

Where the code does branch per platform, both branches are kept honest — the stat
fields that exist only on Unix ([[File metadata fields]]), the token directory
([[Session tokens]]), mount point detection without `/proc` ([[Mount point detection]])
— but nothing exercises the non-Linux ones. Treat macOS and Windows as ''design
targets'': a change should not make a port harder than it is, and should not claim one
exists.

The GUI renders in the system WebView (WebKit2GTK on Linux); which audio and video
codecs it can play depends on the GStreamer plugins installed.

